  {
    "episode_id": 90466,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90466.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Well, my mom, a brave soul and had to swallow her pride and courage and applied for welfare and food stamps that got her by for a few months and applied for school. And fortunately, she got into a program at Lake Washington Vocational Tech School, what it was called at the time. and to get an accounting degree, which would be two years. And meantime, found a part-time job at another small store that she could put a little bit of food on the table. My dad was a veteran, so he got a little bit of money from the VA. and we struggled. I mean, since the time I was early teenager, I worked at stores, I worked part-time, I worked in the summer, I worked Friday nights when everybody else wasn't so did all of my brothers and sisters because we all had to contribute. But it was tough time because getting welfare is not easy when you are a prideful person. And when I hear people put down people on welfare today, I just think about the look in my mom's eyes when she had to go apply for that, but she needed it. You know what, that little bit of help got us back on our feet. My mom got her degree. She worked hard. We all pitched in, and she got her degree, got a job and was somehow able to get us all through school. All seven kids in my family went on to college and got their degrees, which is amazing. But we had a country who believed in investing in people, no matter who you are or what happened to you or where you come from. We all had work study programs that helped us get jobs. We all had Pell grants and student loans and the support we needed to make it through. And I look at families today who are struggling and I think my country has to be there for you. That's part of our obligation as a nation. And I look at those seven kids now, all adults, all giving back in various ways. for brothers from an attorney to a firefighter to Microsoft in genius innovator and sister who was a teacher and a brother who's a sports writer. I mean, we all contribute",
