        "text": "Public mobilization has significant influence on legislative outcomes.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s side represents morally correct or responsible values.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The success of the outcome reflects collective action rather than elite political maneuvering.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Americans acting collectively are more trustworthy and legitimate actors than political institutions alone.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The term 'irresponsible' carries shared moral weight with the intended audience.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90466,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90466.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 199,
    "turn_text": "I give them credit. Yes. But we have said all along when we passed Obamacare many years ago that this was the first step and we needed to continue to make sure that healthcare was affordable and accessible and to continue to find ways to do that. President Trump has taken a very different approach. He came into office to repeal Obamacare. Not sure why other than he liked the word to repeal Obamacare.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "President Trump came into office intending to repeal Obamacare.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "We passed Obamacare many years ago.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "It is necessary to continue making healthcare affordable and accessible.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Obamacare was the first step toward improving healthcare.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "President Trump's motivation for repealing Obamacare is unclear.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that incremental progress on healthcare policy is valid and necessary.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views continued reform as more important than reversal of prior legislation.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Repealing Obamacare without offering a viable alternative undermines public health goals.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Publicly stating intentions to repeal a law implies a meaningful political commitment.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker considers their party’s position on healthcare to be morally superior to Trump’s.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90466,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90466.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 202,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Okay. So it was a campaign promise, not a policy. And without any meaning behind the words. And since he felt so passionate about that, he has used his office and his administration to actually sabotage Obamacare by threatening not to make the payments that are required to be made to insurance companies that help them pay for some of the sicker folks who have health care. Therefore, insurance companies are saying we're going to have to raise our rates because we don't know what's going to happen and they can't operate on uncertainty. They can't operate by a president who is threatening not to subsidize or make the CSR payments because they have to look at their bottom line and say, well, go under if he pulls out from paying these. So they are now raising their rates across the country. We're also seeing a number of what we're called bear counties where insurance companies were not offering. Again, uncertainty is most often named as the reason for that. So what I have told Lamar Alexander and I think many Republicans and Democrats agree is that we need to bring some stability to the marketplace so that we can make sure that we are working as hard as we can to lower the costs for people to be able to purchase insurance. Lamar and I've been talking over the last several weeks. We're going to start having hearings next week, assuring that those CSR payments are mandatory, that the administration can't just decide not to pay them is certainly part of that.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The claim about the campaign promise was not backed by actual policy implementation.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The president threatened to withhold required payments to insurance companies supporting healthcare for sicker individuals.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Insurance companies are increasing premiums due to uncertainty caused by presidential threats.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Uncertainty is cited as the primary reason for the decline in available insurance options in certain areas ('bear counties').",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a proposal to make CSR payments legally mandatory to ensure market stability.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Lamar Alexander and the speaker have discussed the issue and plan to initiate hearings next week.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Both Republicans and Democrats generally agree on the need for market stability.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The current situation undermines efforts to reduce insurance costs for consumers.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "A functioning healthcare market requires predictable financial commitments from the federal government.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Presidential actions that create uncertainty undermine trust in public institutions and economic planning.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
